{"hits": {"hits": [{"links": {"bibtex": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/11171?format=bibtex", "latex-eu": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/11171?format=latex-eu", "latex-us": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/11171?format=latex-us", "json": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/11171?format=json", "json-expanded": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/11171?format=json-expanded", "cv": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/11171?format=cv", "citations": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A11171"}, "updated": "2023-03-10T14:12:21.413458+00:00", "id": "11171", "created": "1981-10-01T00:00:00+00:00", "metadata": {"publication_info": [{"journal_volume": "197", "page_end": "476", "year": 1982, "page_start": "446", "journal_title": "Nucl.Phys.B"}], "authors": [{"full_name": "Collins, John C."}, {"full_name": "Soper, Davison E."}], "texkeys": ["Collins:1981va"], "imprints": [{"date": "1982"}], "abstracts": [{"value": "In an earlier paper, we derived an algorithm for calculating the cross section for e + +e \u2212 \u2192 A + B + X, with hadrons A and B nearly back to back. The result was given in transverse position ( b ) space for the Fourier transform of the small angle part of the cross section. Here we discuss the structure of this result, particularly at large b , where purely perturbative calculations are not useful. We then describe how to return from b space to transverse momentum space."}], "control_number": 11171, "dois": [{"value": "10.1016/0550-3213(82)90453-9"}], "titles": [{"title": "Back-To-Back Jets: Fourier Transform from B to K-Transverse"}, {"title": "BACK TO BACK"}], "author_count": 2, "document_type": ["article"]}}], "total": 1}, "links": {"self": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?sort=mostcited&q=j%20Nucl.Phys.B%2C197%2C446&size=3&page=1&fields=control_number,titles.title,authors.full_name,author_count,publication_info,arxiv_eprints.value,dois.value,texkeys,abstracts.value,document_type,imprints,isbns